<phyphox version="1.14" locale="en">
  <title>Sound Level Meter (A/C/Z)</title>
  <category>Acoustics</category>
  <icon>dB</icon>
  <description>
        WHAT IT DOES
        Measures how loud it is, the way noise rules measure it.

        WHY YOU CARE
        Loudness is what turns up in complaints, in commissioning paperwork, and in arguments about hearing protection. And a room that gets louder over months is a machine going wrong.

        THE THREE LETTERS
        A, C and Z are three ways of adding the noise up.
        A is what everyone means by decibels. It ignores deep rumble, because human ears largely do.
        C keeps the rumble in.
        Z is raw, nothing ignored.

        A trick worth knowing: look at C minus A. A BIG gap means the noise is mostly low rumble, so fans, transformers or structure. A SMALL gap means it is hiss and whine. That tells you what kind of problem you have before you have done anything else.

        HOW TO RUN IT
        1. Press play.
        2. Hold the phone at head height, arm out, away from your body. Your chest reflects sound and skews it.
        3. Wait at least 30 seconds.
        4. Read Leq, not the jumpy live number. Leq is the average since you pressed play, and it is the one to write down.

        THE CATCH, AND IT IS A REAL ONE
        Your phone has no idea how loud a sound truly is. It measures a signal, and turning that signal into decibels needs a fixed number added on. This starts at 120, which is a plausible guess and nothing more.

        What that means in practice:
        Comparing two places works perfectly right now, because the guess cancels out on both sides.
        Watching one place change over time works right now, same reason.
        The single absolute number is a guess until you calibrate. See the Calibration tab.

        NEVER USE IT FOR
        Hearing protection decisions, or anything with a legal limit. Phones distort above roughly 100 dB and a phone microphone is not a measuring instrument. If someone's hearing depends on the answer, borrow a real meter.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <!-- audio input -->
    <container size="16384">recording</container>
    <container>rate</container>
    <container init="4096">samples</container>
    <container size="4096">block</container>
    <container size="16384">remainder</container>

    <!-- block geometry -->
    <container>n</container>
    <container>nHalf</container>
    <container>nHalf1</container>
    <container>rateHalf</container>

    <!-- Hann window -->
    <container size="4096">winU</container>
    <container size="4096">window</container>
    <container size="4096">windowed</container>

    <!-- spectrum -->
    <container size="4096">fftRe</container>
    <container size="4096">fftIm</container>
    <container size="2049">freq</container>
    <container size="2048">halfF</container>
    <container size="2048">halfRe</container>
    <container size="2048">halfIm</container>
    <container size="2048">pw</container>
    <container size="2048">wA</container>
    <container size="2048">wC</container>
    <container size="2048">pwA</container>
    <container size="2048">pwC</container>

    <!-- band sums and mean squares -->
    <container>sumZ</container>
    <container>sumA</container>
    <container>sumC</container>
    <container>msZ</container>
    <container>msA</container>
    <container>msC</container>
    <container>lnZ</container>
    <container>lnA</container>
    <container>lnC</container>

    <!-- calibration -->
    <container init="120">calibration</container>
    <container init="0">calibrated</container>
    <container>userCalibration</container>
    <container init="85">targetDB</container>
    <container>newCal</container>

    <!-- levels -->
    <container size="0">LZ</container>
    <container size="0">LA</container>
    <container size="0">LC</container>
    <container>LAmax</container>
    <container>LAmin</container>
    <container>CminusA</container>

    <!-- statistical levels: sort the whole LA history and pick percentiles -->
    <container size="0">sortedLA</container>
    <container>nLevels</container>
    <container>i90</container>
    <container>i50</container>
    <container>i10</container>
    <container>LA90</container>
    <container>LA50</container>
    <container>LA10</container>

    <!-- 1 s rolling average (12 blocks) -->
    <container size="12">slowRing</container>
    <container>msASlow</container>
    <container>lnASlow</container>
    <container>dbASlowRaw</container>
    <container>LAslow</container>

    <!-- Leq over the whole run -->
    <container size="0">msAall</container>
    <container>msAeq</container>
    <container>lnAeq</container>
    <container>dbAeqRaw</container>
    <container>LAeq</container>

    <!-- peak / clipping -->
    <container size="4096">absBlock</container>
    <container>peakAmp</container>
    <container>peakSq</container>
    <container>lnPeak</container>
    <container size="0">peakRawHist</container>
    <container>peakRawMax</container>
    <container>LpeakMax</container>

    <!-- time base -->
    <container>now</container>
    <container init="0">t0</container>
    <container init="0">resetTime</container>
    <container size="0">time</container>
    <container>tNow</container>

    <!-- exposure -->
    <container init="85">criterion</container>
    <container>tFraction</container>
    <container>lnTFraction</container>
    <container>lex8</container>
    <container>dose</container>
    <container>tAllowed</container>
    <container>tRemain</container>

    <!-- spectrum display -->
    <container size="2048">specMs</container>
    <container size="2048">specLn</container>
    <container size="2048">specDb</container>
  </data-containers>

  <input>
    <audio append="true">
      <output>recording</output>
      <output component="rate">rate</output>
    </audio>
  </input>

  <views>
    <view label="Meter">
      <value label="Calibration status">
        <input>calibrated</input>
        <map min="0" max="0">DEFAULT OFFSET - not calibrated</map>
        <map>calibrated</map>
      </value>
      <value label="Clipping check">
        <input>peakRawMax</input>
        <map min="-0.05">CLIPPED - readings are invalid</map>
        <map min="-3">Close to clipping - move away from the source</map>
        <map>OK</map>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="LA (loudness the way ears hear it, last 85 ms)" unit="dB(A)" size="3" precision="1">
        <input>LA</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LA slow (same thing, averaged over 1 second)" unit="dB(A)" size="2" precision="1">
        <input>LAslow</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="LAeq (average loudness since you pressed play)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LAeq</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LAmax (the loudest single moment)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LAmax</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LAmin (the quietest single moment)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LAmin</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Lpeak max (biggest single spike, nothing ignored)" unit="dB" precision="1">
        <input>LpeakMax</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="LA90 (the quiet background between events)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LA90</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LA50 (the middle level - half the time above, half below)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LA50</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LA10 (the intrusive peaks - exceeded a tenth of the time)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LA10</input>
      </value>
      <info label="These three describe the SHAPE of the noise over time, not just the average. LA90 is the level the noise stays above 90% of the time - it is the steady background, and it is what the BS 4142 rules judge a new noise against. LA10 is where the loud events poke through. A big gap between LA10 and LA90 means spiky, event-driven noise; a small gap means a steady drone. Let it run a minute or two before trusting them."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="LC (loudness with the deep rumble kept in)" unit="dB(C)" precision="1">
        <input>LC</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LZ (loudness raw, nothing ignored)" unit="dB" precision="1">
        <input>LZ</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LC minus LA (the deep-rumble gap)" unit="dB" precision="1">
        <input>CminusA</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Character">
        <input>CminusA</input>
        <map max="3">little low-frequency energy</map>
        <map max="10">some low-frequency energy</map>
        <map max="20">low-frequency dominated (rumble)</map>
        <map>strongly low-frequency dominated</map>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="History" labelX="Time" unitX="s" labelY="Sound level" unitY="dB(A)" partialUpdate="true">
        <input axis="x">time</input>
        <input axis="y">LA</input>
      </graph>
      <button label="Reset history and Leq">
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>LA</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>LC</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>LZ</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>time</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>msAall</output>
        <input type="empty"/>
        <output>peakRawHist</output>
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>resetTime</output>
      </button>
    </view>

    <view label="Spectrum">
      <info label="This is the spectrum - a list of which pitches the sound is made of. Each point is one narrow pitch slice, called an FFT bin, about 11.7 Hz (times per second) wide here. A peak, a pitch louder than its neighbours, points to one source: a fan blade passing, transformer hum, or coil whine."/>
      <graph label="Spectrum" logX="true" labelX="Frequency" unitX="Hz" labelY="Bin level" unitY="dB" partialUpdate="false">
        <input axis="x">halfF</input>
        <input axis="y">specDb</input>
      </graph>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="LZ (loudness raw, nothing ignored, whole range)" unit="dB" precision="1">
        <input>LZ</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Sample rate" unit="Hz" precision="0">
        <input>rate</input>
      </value>
    </view>

    <view label="Exposure">
      <info label="This adds up your noise dose over a full 8 hour work day. It uses a 3 dB exchange rate: every extra 3 dB halves the safe time. Set the criterion level below - the loudness limit you are checking against. 85 dB(A) is the usual action level, the point where you are meant to act."/>
      <info label="For the curious: this follows the ISO 9612 and NIOSH methods."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Measurement time" unit="s" precision="0">
        <input>tNow</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LAeq so far" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>LAeq</input>
      </value>
      <value label="LEX,8h (this loudness stretched over a full 8 hour day, as one number)" unit="dB(A)" precision="1">
        <input>lex8</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Dose accumulated so far" unit="%" precision="2">
        <input>dose</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Allowed time at this level" unit="h" factor="0.000277777777777778" precision="2">
        <input>tAllowed</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Time left before 100 % dose" unit="h" factor="0.000277777777777778" precision="2">
        <input>tRemain</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <edit label="Criterion level (the loudness limit you check against)" unit="dB(A)" default="85" decimal="true">
        <output>criterion</output>
      </edit>
    </view>

    <view label="Calibration">
      <info label="WHAT CALIBRATION MEANS HERE"/>
      <info label="The phone measures a signal, not a sound level. To turn one into the other, a fixed number is added on. That number is the offset below. It starts at 120, which is a plausible value for a typical phone and is NOT a measurement."/>
      <info label="Everything comparative already works without doing this. Two places, or one place across two months, both cancel the offset out. You only need to calibrate if you want to quote a single number with dB after it."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHERE TO GET A REFERENCE WITHOUT BUYING ONE"/>
      <info label="Ask whoever does hearing checks on site - safety, industrial hygiene, facilities. They own a proper meter, and that is your reference - the known-good thing you score the phone against. It is calibrated every year because it has to be. Ten minutes standing next to them is the whole job."/>
      <info label="Failing that: an existing noise survey for the building gives you numbers at known spots. Stand there and match. Or borrow from an acoustic or HVAC contractor. A basic meter is also only about thirty pounds or dollars new, which surprises people."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="HOW TO DO IT, STEP BY STEP"/>
      <info label="1. Stand in noise of about the level you actually care about, so 75 to 85 dB in a hall. Do NOT do this in a quiet office - phone microphones misbehave at the extremes and you would be calibrating the wrong end of the scale."/>
      <info label="2. Put the reference meter and the phone side by side, same height, microphones pointing the same way."/>
      <info label="3. Let both settle for 30 seconds. Averaged against averaged, never a jumping number against a jumping number."/>
      <info label="4. Read the reference in dB(A), type it into Reference level below, and press Calibrate while this experiment is running."/>
      <info label="5. WRITE THE RESULTING OFFSET DOWN. It is not saved between sessions, and it is the whole result of the exercise."/>
      <edit label="Reference level" unit="dB(A)" default="85" decimal="true">
        <output>targetDB</output>
      </edit>
      <button label="Calibrate">
        <input>newCal</input>
        <output>calibration</output>
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>calibrated</output>
      </button>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Current offset (the fixed number added to make real decibels)" unit="dB" precision="2">
        <input>calibration</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Uncalibrated 1 s level (dBFS: loudness against the loudest the phone can record, so always a minus number)" unit="dBFS" precision="2">
        <input>dbASlowRaw</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="If you already know the offset for this phone from an earlier calibration, enter it directly and press Set offset. Write it down - it is not stored between sessions."/>
      <edit label="Known offset" unit="dB" default="120" decimal="true" signed="true">
        <output>userCalibration</output>
      </edit>
      <button label="Set offset">
        <input>userCalibration</input>
        <output>calibration</output>
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>calibrated</output>
      </button>
    </view>

    <view label="Notes">
      <info label="WHICH NUMBER DO I ACTUALLY WRITE DOWN"/>
      <info label="Leq. It is the average since you pressed play, and it is what noise reports quote. The big live number jumps around far too much to mean anything on its own - quote it and the next person to measure will get something different and think you were wrong."/>
      <info label="Lmax and Lmin are the loudest and quietest moments. Useful for spotting a door slam or a fan surge inside an otherwise steady reading."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHAT DECIBELS ACTUALLY ARE, BRIEFLY"/>
      <info label="They are not a normal scale. Every 10 dB is TEN TIMES the sound energy, and roughly twice as loud to a human ear. So 70 and 80 are not a small difference - the second one has ten times the energy of the first."/>
      <info label="This also means you cannot add them like ordinary numbers. Two machines at 70 dB each give about 73 dB together, not 140."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="HOW TO HOLD IT SO THE READING MEANS SOMETHING"/>
      <info label="Head height, arm extended, microphone pointing into the room. Your body reflects sound, so a phone held against your chest reads high. Keep it in the same position every time or you cannot compare two readings."/>
      <info label="Take the case off, or at least know where the microphone is - usually the bottom edge near the charging port. A case lip over it can cost several dB, and it will cost the same several dB every time, silently."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="THE FIVE WAYS THIS DIFFERS FROM A PROPER METER"/>
      <info label="1. The microphone is uncalibrated, and how it responds to different pitches is unknown. Most phones are poor below about 100 Hz and above 10 kHz, and cut bass sharply. In a hall full of low fan rumble that matters."/>
      <info label="2. A real meter averages with a smooth decay, defined by standard. This averages in flat blocks, 85 milliseconds and 1 second. Close, not identical."/>
      <info label="3. The peak reading here is simply the largest single sample. A real meter measures peak differently and to a defined standard."/>
      <info label="4. Your phone may quietly turn its own microphone gain up and down - it is built to make voices sound even, which is the opposite of what measuring needs. If the reading slides downward while a steady noise continues, that is what is happening, and NO calibration can fix it."/>
      <info label="5. Above roughly 100 to 110 dB the input clips - the signal is simply too big to record and the tops get cut off. Everything above that reads too low. Watch the clipping indicator."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHAT IT IS GOOD FOR"/>
      <info label="Comparing places. Watching one place over time. Finding which pitch a noise sits at. Deciding where to send someone with a proper meter."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHAT IT IS NOT FOR"/>
      <info label="Hearing protection, legal limits, or anything where being wrong has consequences. This is a phone."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WORDS THIS USES"/>
      <info label="Hz - times per second. kHz means thousands of times per second."/>
      <info label="decibel (dB) - a loudness scale. Every 10 dB is ten times the sound energy, and about twice as loud to your ears."/>
      <info label="A, C and Z - three ways of adding the noise up. A ignores deep rumble the way ears do. C keeps the rumble in. Z is raw, nothing ignored."/>
      <info label="LA, LC, LZ - the loudness measured the A, C or Z way."/>
      <info label="Leq and LAeq - the average loudness since you pressed play. LAeq is that average done the A way."/>
      <info label="Lpeak - the single biggest spike of sound, not an average."/>
      <info label="LAmax and LAmin - the loudest and quietest single moments."/>
      <info label="spectrum - a list of which pitches a sound is made of. A peak is a pitch louder than its neighbours."/>
      <info label="FFT bin - one narrow pitch slice of that list."/>
      <info label="dBFS - loudness compared with the loudest the phone can record, so always a minus number."/>
      <info label="offset (calibrate) - a fixed number added to turn the phone's raw signal into real decibels."/>
      <info label="reference - the known-good meter you score the phone against."/>
      <info label="exchange rate - how much extra loudness halves your safe time. Here every 3 dB halves it."/>
      <info label="criterion or action level - the loudness limit you are checking against."/>
      <info label="LEX,8h - this loudness stretched over a full 8 hour day, written as one number."/>
      <info label="dose - how much of your safe daily noise you have used, as a percent."/>
    </view>
  </views>

  <analysis requireFill="recording" requireFillDynamic="samples">

    <!-- time base -->
    <timer>
      <output clear="false">now</output>
    </timer>
    <if equal="true">
      <input clear="false">resetTime</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <input clear="false">now</input>
      <output>t0</output>
    </if>
    <append>
      <input type="value">0</input>
      <output>resetTime</output>
    </append>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">now</input>
      <input clear="false">t0</input>
      <output clear="false">time</output>
    </subtract>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">now</input>
      <input clear="false">t0</input>
      <output>tNow</output>
    </subtract>

    <!-- take one block, keep the remainder -->
    <subrange>
      <input as="to" clear="false">samples</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">recording</input>
      <output>block</output>
    </subrange>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">samples</input>
      <input as="in" clear="true">recording</input>
      <output>remainder</output>
    </subrange>
    <subrange>
      <input>remainder</input>
      <output>recording</output>
    </subrange>
    <count>
      <input clear="false">block</input>
      <output>n</output>
    </count>

    <!-- peak of the raw block (clipping check) -->
    <formula formula="abs([1_])">
      <input clear="false">block</input>
      <output>absBlock</output>
    </formula>
    <max>
      <input as="y">absBlock</input>
      <output as="max">peakAmp</output>
    </max>
    <formula formula="[1]*[1]+0.000000000001">
      <input>peakAmp</input>
      <output>peakSq</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input>peakSq</input>
      <output>lnPeak</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]">
      <input>lnPeak</input>
      <output clear="false">peakRawHist</output>
    </formula>
    <max>
      <input as="y" clear="false">peakRawHist</input>
      <output as="max">peakRawMax</output>
    </max>
    <formula formula="[1]+[2]">
      <input clear="false">peakRawMax</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output>LpeakMax</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- Hann window -->
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">n</input>
      <output>winU</output>
    </ramp>
    <formula formula="0.5-0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*[1_])">
      <input>winU</input>
      <output>window</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor">block</input>
      <input as="factor">window</input>
      <output>windowed</output>
    </multiply>

    <!-- FFT and frequency axis -->
    <fft>
      <input>windowed</input>
      <output as="re">fftRe</output>
      <output as="im">fftIm</output>
    </fft>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">rate</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output>rateHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output>nHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <add>
      <input clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output>nHalf1</output>
    </add>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" clear="false">rateHalf</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">nHalf1</input>
      <output>freq</output>
    </ramp>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input as="in">freq</input>
      <input as="in">fftRe</input>
      <input as="in">fftIm</input>
      <output>halfF</output>
      <output>halfRe</output>
      <output>halfIm</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="[1_]*[1_]+[2_]*[2_]">
      <input>halfRe</input>
      <input>halfIm</input>
      <output>pw</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- IEC 61672 weighting curves, squared -->
    <formula formula="1.584841544872967*148693636^2*[1_]^8/(([1_]^2+424.36)^2*([1_]^2+11599.29)*([1_]^2+544496.41)*([1_]^2+148693636)^2)">
      <input clear="false">halfF</input>
      <output>wA</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="1.0143560601767088*148693636^2*[1_]^4/(([1_]^2+424.36)^2*([1_]^2+148693636)^2)">
      <input clear="false">halfF</input>
      <output>wC</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">pw</input>
      <input as="factor">wA</input>
      <output>pwA</output>
    </multiply>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">pw</input>
      <input as="factor">wC</input>
      <output>pwC</output>
    </multiply>

    <!-- integrate each spectrum to a mean square -->
    <integrate>
      <input clear="false">pw</input>
      <output>sumZ</output>
    </integrate>
    <integrate>
      <input>pwA</input>
      <output>sumA</output>
    </integrate>
    <integrate>
      <input>pwC</input>
      <output>sumC</output>
    </integrate>
    <!-- 2 (half spectrum) / 0.375 (Hann power) / n^2 (DFT scale) = 5.3333/n^2 -->
    <formula formula="5.333333333333333*[1]/([2]*[2])+0.000000000000000001">
      <input>sumZ</input>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <output>msZ</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="5.333333333333333*[1]/([2]*[2])+0.000000000000000001">
      <input>sumA</input>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <output clear="false">msA</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="5.333333333333333*[1]/([2]*[2])+0.000000000000000001">
      <input>sumC</input>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <output>msC</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- levels in dB -->
    <log>
      <input>msZ</input>
      <output>lnZ</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]+[2]">
      <input>lnZ</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output clear="false">LZ</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input clear="false">msA</input>
      <output>lnA</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]+[2]">
      <input>lnA</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output clear="false">LA</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input>msC</input>
      <output>lnC</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]+[2]">
      <input>lnC</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output clear="false">LC</output>
    </formula>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">LC</input>
      <input clear="false">LA</input>
      <output>CminusA</output>
    </subtract>
    <max>
      <input as="y" clear="false">LA</input>
      <output as="max">LAmax</output>
    </max>
    <min>
      <input as="y" clear="false">LA</input>
      <output as="min">LAmin</output>
    </min>

    <!-- statistical levels. Sort the whole level history low-to-high, then read
         off percentiles. L90 (exceeded 90% of the time) sits at the 10th
         percentile - it is the quiet background between events, the number
         BS 4142 assesses noise against. L10 (exceeded 10%) is the intrusive
         peaks. L50 is the median. -->
    <sort>
      <input clear="false">LA</input>
      <output>sortedLA</output>
    </sort>
    <count>
      <input clear="false">LA</input>
      <output>nLevels</output>
    </count>
    <formula formula="max(round(0.1*[1])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">nLevels</input>
      <output>i90</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="max(round(0.5*[1])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">nLevels</input>
      <output>i50</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="max(round(0.9*[1])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">nLevels</input>
      <output>i10</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">i90</input>
      <input as="length" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">sortedLA</input>
      <output>LA90</output>
    </subrange>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">i50</input>
      <input as="length" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">sortedLA</input>
      <output>LA50</output>
    </subrange>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">i10</input>
      <input as="length" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">sortedLA</input>
      <output>LA10</output>
    </subrange>

    <!-- 1 s rolling average -->
    <append>
      <input clear="false">msA</input>
      <output clear="false">slowRing</output>
    </append>
    <average>
      <input as="buffer" clear="false">slowRing</input>
      <output as="average">msASlow</output>
    </average>
    <log>
      <input>msASlow</input>
      <output>lnASlow</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]">
      <input>lnASlow</input>
      <output clear="false">dbASlowRaw</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="[1]+[2]">
      <input clear="false">dbASlowRaw</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output>LAslow</output>
    </formula>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">targetDB</input>
      <input clear="false">dbASlowRaw</input>
      <output>newCal</output>
    </subtract>

    <!-- Leq over the whole run -->
    <append>
      <input clear="false">msA</input>
      <output clear="false">msAall</output>
    </append>
    <average>
      <input as="buffer" clear="false">msAall</input>
      <output as="average">msAeq</output>
    </average>
    <log>
      <input>msAeq</input>
      <output>lnAeq</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]">
      <input>lnAeq</input>
      <output>dbAeqRaw</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="[1]+[2]">
      <input>dbAeqRaw</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output clear="false">LAeq</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- exposure, 3 dB exchange rate over 8 h -->
    <formula formula="max([1],0.001)/28800">
      <input clear="false">tNow</input>
      <output>tFraction</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input>tFraction</input>
      <output>lnTFraction</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="[1]+4.342944819032518*[2]">
      <input clear="false">LAeq</input>
      <input>lnTFraction</input>
      <output>lex8</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="100*[2]/28800*10^(([1]-[3])/10)">
      <input clear="false">LAeq</input>
      <input clear="false">tNow</input>
      <input clear="false">criterion</input>
      <output>dose</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="28800*10^(([2]-[1])/10)">
      <input clear="false">LAeq</input>
      <input clear="false">criterion</input>
      <output clear="false">tAllowed</output>
    </formula>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">tAllowed</input>
      <input clear="false">tNow</input>
      <output>tRemain</output>
    </subtract>

    <!-- spectrum for display -->
    <formula formula="5.333333333333333*[1_]/([2]*[2])+0.000000000000000001">
      <input>pw</input>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <output>specMs</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input>specMs</input>
      <output>specLn</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1_]+[2]">
      <input>specLn</input>
      <input clear="false">calibration</input>
      <output>specDb</output>
    </formula>

  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Levels">
      <data name="Time (s)">time</data>
      <data name="LA (dB(A))">LA</data>
      <data name="LC (dB(C))">LC</data>
      <data name="LZ (dB)">LZ</data>
      <data name="Peak, uncalibrated (dBFS)">peakRawHist</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Summary">
      <data name="LAeq (dB(A))">LAeq</data>
      <data name="LAmax (dB(A))">LAmax</data>
      <data name="LAmin (dB(A))">LAmin</data>
      <data name="LEX,8h (dB(A))">lex8</data>
      <data name="Dose (%)">dose</data>
      <data name="Measurement time (s)">tNow</data>
      <data name="Calibration offset (dB)">calibration</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Spectrum">
      <data name="Frequency (Hz)">halfF</data>
      <data name="Bin level (dB)">specDb</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
